From: Rahul Rameshbabu <rrameshbabu@nvidia.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Sep 2025 16:02:19 -0700
Subject: backends/monitor-manager: Use normal modes when tiles are missing
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Originally, Meta.MonitorManager would handle tiled monitors without
gracefully handling the case where the main tile in the topology is
missing. This is problematic since, when setting the tiled topology, a
modeset will not be performed without the presence of the main tile. The
impact of this to an end user is having a black unusable screen on the
monitor. The issue is further emphasized by the fact that any detection of
tiled monitor support will try defaulting to tiling modes instead of normal
modes in the Meta.MonitorManager design, meaning the starting experience
using Mutter with such a monitor setup is a black unusable screen.

The situation where the main tile can be missing is when a monitor has
multiple physical connections. One connection has the main tile
characteristics in its EDID while the others have locations that
characterize them as not the main tile in the topology. If a user connects
to a port on the monitor that is not the main tile, no modeset is performed
and a black unusable screen is seen on the display.

